    "fog": {
      "word": "FOG",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "(Agric.) To pasture cattle on the fog, or aftergrass, of; to eat off the fog from.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To practice in a small or mean way; to pettifog. [Obs.] Where wouldst thou fog to get a fee Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "Watery vapor condensed in the lower part of the atmosphere and disturbing its transparency. It differs from cloud only in being near the ground, and from mist in not approaching so nearly to fine rain. See Cloud.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A state of mental confusion. Fog alarm, Fog bell, Fog horn, etc., a bell, horn, whistle or other contrivance that sounds an alarm, often automatically, near places of danger where visible signals would be hidden in thick weather. distant land. seen on the coast of Newfoundland.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To envelop, as with fog; to befog; to overcast; to darken; to obscure.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To show indistinctly or become indistinct, as the picture on a negative sometimes does in the process of development.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
